\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"20:Ta64,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"{\\\"@context\\\":\\\"https://schema.org\\\",\\\"@graph\\\":[{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison#author\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Person\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Rachel Mitchell\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/about#rachel-mitchell\\\",\\\"jobTitle\\\":\\\"Lead Tax Analyst \\u0026 Editorial Director, CPA\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Rachel Mitchell is a Certified Public Accountant (CPA) licensed in Illinois with over 12 years of experience in individual and small-business taxation. She specializes in federal and state income tax compliance, FICA optimization, payroll tax strategy, and multi-state tax planning. Rachel holds an MS in Taxation from Golden Gate University and a BS in Accounting from the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. She is an active member of the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA) and the Illinois CPA Society. Before joining TheTaxCalc, Rachel spent 8 years at a Big Four accounting firm advising high-net-worth clients on tax-efficient wealth strategies.\\\",\\\"knowsAbout\\\":[\\\"Federal Income Tax\\\",\\\"State Income Tax\\\",\\\"FICA Tax\\\",\\\"Payroll Tax\\\",\\\"IRS Compliance\\\",\\\"Tax Planning\\\",\\\"Multi-State Taxation\\\",\\\"Tax-Advantaged Accounts\\\"],\\\"sameAs\\\":[\\\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rachelmitchellcpa/\\\",\\\"https://www.aicpa-cima.com/\\\"],\\\"worksFor\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com\\\"},\\\"image\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/author-rachel-mitchell.webp\\\"},{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison#article\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Article\\\",\\\"headline\\\":\\\"Florida vs Texas Tax Comparison\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"A detailed comparison of the tax structures in Florida and Texas — two of the most popular no-income-tax states for relocation.\\\",\\\"image\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ImageObject\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/opengraph-image.png\\\",\\\"width\\\":1200,\\\"height\\\":630},\\\"datePublished\\\":\\\"2026-05-26T06:59:34.069Z\\\",\\\"dateModified\\\":\\\"2026-05-26T20:31:33.169Z\\\",\\\"author\\\":{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison#author\\\"},\\\"publisher\\\":{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/#organization\\\"},\\\"mainEntityOfPage\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"WebPage\\\",\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison\\\"},\\\"keywords\\\":[\\\"florida\\\",\\\"texas\\\",\\\"tax comparison\\\",\\\"no income tax\\\",\\\"relocation\\\"],\\\"wordCount\\\":1271,\\\"articleSection\\\":\\\"Comparison\\\"},{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison#breadcrumb\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"BreadcrumbList\\\",\\\"itemListElement\\\":[{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ListItem\\\",\\\"position\\\":1,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Home\\\",\\\"item\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com\\\"},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ListItem\\\",\\\"position\\\":2,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Blog\\\",\\\"item\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog\\\"},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ListItem\\\",\\\"position\\\":3,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Florida vs Texas Tax Comparison\\\",\\\"item\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison\\\"}]}]}\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"17:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 lg:px-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"script\\\",null,{\\\"type\\\":\\\"application/ld+json\\\",\\\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\\\":{\\\"__html\\\":\\\"$20\\\"}}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"aria-label\\\":\\\"Breadcrumb\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-6 flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"hover:text-foreground transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Home\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":\\\"true\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"/\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"hover:text-foreground transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Blog\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":\\\"true\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"/\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"truncate text-foreground font-medium max-w-[200px] sm:max-w-none\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Florida vs Texas Tax Comparison\\\"}]]}],\\\"$L21\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",\\\"$L23\\\",\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\"]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"26:I[45026,[\\\"/_next/static/chunks/49e33bee669819de.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/868a9c4503c53c54.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/d791dd6e73034ab4.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/2d86c01f2aacd453.js\\\"],\\\"BlogTableOfContents\\\"]\\n27:T210c,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"\\u003cp\\u003eI've had this conversation probably a dozen times in the last two years. Friends, coworkers, random people at BBQs — everyone moving out of California or New York or Illinois ends up asking the same question: \\\"Should I go to Florida or Texas?\\\"\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAnd look, I get why it comes down to these two. They're both massive states with no income tax, both warm (okay, hot), both relatively affordable compared to the coasts. But the tax pictures are way more different than most people realize, and picking the wrong one can cost you thousands.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLet me walk you through it — not with a bunch of tables and \\\"Winner:\\\" labels, but like an actual conversation. Because I've helped friends do this math for real.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"income-tax-they-re-both-at-zero\\\"\\u003eIncome Tax: They're Both at Zero\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis is the easy part. Neither Florida nor Texas has a personal income tax. Zero. Zilch. No state tax on your wages, your self-employment income, your dividends, your capital gains, your 401(k) distributions — none of it.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSo on income tax alone, these two states are dead even. If that's all you care about, flip a coin. But you should care about the other stuff, because that's where the real differences live.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"property-taxes-and-this-is-where-florida-pulls-ahead\\\"\\u003eProperty Taxes — And This Is Where Florida Pulls Ahead\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOkay, this is the big one. The property tax gap between these states is massive.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFlorida's average effective property tax rate is about \\u003cstrong\\u003e0.86%\\u003c/strong\\u003e of your home's value (our \\u003ca href=\\\"/florida-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eFlorida calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e factors this in). Texas? Roughly \\u003cstrong\\u003e1.60%\\u003c/strong\\u003e (the \\u003ca href=\\\"/texas-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eTexas calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e shows the full breakdown). That's nearly double. On a $350,000 house, you're looking at around $3,010 a year in Florida versus something like $5,600 in Texas. Same house. Two thousand six hundred dollars more. Every single year.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAnd it gets worse for Texas when you dig into the details. Florida has this thing called the \\u003cstrong\\u003ehomestead exemption\\u003c/strong\\u003e — it knocks $25,000 to $50,000 off your home's assessed value before they even calculate the tax. Plus Florida's \\\"Save Our Homes\\\" cap limits how much your assessed value can go up each year to 3% or the CPI, whichever is less. So if you buy a house and values skyrocket around you, your tax bill doesn't explode along with them.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eTexas? No statewide homestead exemption. No assessment cap like Florida's. (Some areas have a 10% appraisal cap, but that's still way more than 3%.) I've heard stories from friends in the DFW suburbs whose property tax bills jumped 15-20% in a single year because their neighborhood got hot. That stings.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFlorida takes this one, hands down. If you're buying a house, the property tax difference alone could be the deciding factor.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"sales-tax-texas-has-a-slight-edge-here\\\"\\u003eSales Tax — Texas Has a Slight Edge Here\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFlorida's state sales tax is 6.00%, and local add-ons can push the total up to 8.50%. Texas charges 6.25% at the state level with a max combined rate of 8.25%. On average, you'll pay about 7.02% in Florida and 8.19% in Texas.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSo wait — didn't I say Texas has the edge? I did, and here's why: Florida's \\u003cem\\u003emaximum\\u003c/em\\u003e combined rate is technically higher than Texas', and in the big tourist areas (Orlando, Miami), you'll regularly hit that 7.5-8.5% range. But overall, Texas' average combined rate is actually a bit higher. Honestly? The difference is small enough that it shouldn't drive your decision. We're talking maybe a few hundred bucks a year for most people.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eBoth states exempt groceries, by the way. So at least you're not paying sales tax on food in either place.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"the-hidden-costs-people-forget\\\"\\u003eThe Hidden Costs People Forget\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHere's where it gets interesting, and where a lot of comparisons fall short.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eHomeowners insurance in Florida is brutal.\\u003c/strong\\u003e Like, genuinely painful. Because hurricanes. Because the insurance market down there is... well, let's just say it's been having some issues. Florida homeowners insurance can easily run $3,000 to $6,000+ more per year than in Texas. On a similar house. That's not a small number, and it eats into the property tax savings fast.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFlorida also has a documentary stamp tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e on real estate transfers — $0.70 per $100 of the sale price. Buying a $400,000 house? That's $2,800 in stamp taxes at closing. Texas doesn't have that. There's also a small intangible tax on certain investments in Florida (0.002% — basically nothing) and a tourist development tax on short-term rentals (6%, which only matters if you're doing Airbnb stuff).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTexas has the franchise tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e on businesses — 0.331% to 0.75% on margins. If you're a small business owner, that's something to factor in. Florida doesn't have an equivalent for most small businesses.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"housing-costs-the-other-big-difference\\\"\\u003eHousing Costs — The Other Big Difference\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eMedian home prices tell a story: roughly $390,000 in Florida versus around $305,000 in Texas. If you're financing, our \\u003ca href=\\\"/mortgage-calculator\\\"\\u003emortgage calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e can show you what those payments look like. Florida's more expensive to buy into, especially anywhere near the coast. And HOA fees in Florida tend to run higher, particularly in communities with amenities (which is like... most of South Florida).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSo even though Florida's property tax \\u003cem\\u003erate\\u003c/em\\u003e is lower, you might be paying it on a more expensive house. The math gets complicated. Trust me.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"let-s-run-some-real-numbers\\\"\\u003eLet's Run Some Real Numbers\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eI did this exact calculation for my coworker Marcus back in November. He was making $100,000 and trying to decide between Tampa and Dallas.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFlorida scenario\\u003c/strong\\u003e (roughly):\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eProperty tax on a median home: about $3,200/year\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSales tax on typical spending: around $2,100/year\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eTotal state and local tax burden: roughly \\u003cstrong\\u003e$5,300\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTexas scenario\\u003c/strong\\u003e (roughly):\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eProperty tax on a median home: about $5,600/year\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSales tax on typical spending: around $2,800/year\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eTotal: roughly \\u003cstrong\\u003e$8,400\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSo on a straight tax comparison, Florida was about $3,100 cheaper per year. But then we factored in insurance — his Florida quote was $4,200 more annually than the Texas one. And suddenly the gap narrowed to basically nothing.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor a family making $200,000 with a $500,000 home, the picture looks something like this:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFlorida\\u003c/strong\\u003e: roughly $4,300 in property tax, $3,500 in sales tax, plus that killer insurance — you're looking at \\u003cstrong\\u003e$11,000+\\u003c/strong\\u003e total.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTexas\\u003c/strong\\u003e: roughly $8,000 in property tax, $4,200 in sales tax, but moderate insurance — somewhere around \\u003cstrong\\u003e$12,800\\u003c/strong\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFlorida still comes out a bit ahead tax-wise, but the insurance situation in Florida is a real wildcard. It varies wildly by location, by year, by insurer. Some of my Florida friends have seen their premiums double in two years. That's not sustainable.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"so-which-one\\\"\\u003eSo Which One?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eI genuinely can't give you a clean answer because it depends so much on your situation — our \\u003ca href=\\\"/relocation-calculator\\\"\\u003erelocation calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e can run the numbers for your specific scenario. But here's my honest take after helping several people through this:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFlorida probably makes more sense if\\u003c/strong\\u003e you're buying a home and planning to stay (that Save Our Homes cap is incredibly valuable over time), you're a retiree (Florida doesn't tax anything and the homestead exemption is great), or you just really want to live near the ocean and don't mind the insurance costs.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTexas probably makes more sense if\\u003c/strong\\u003e you're renting (you dodge the property tax bullet entirely), you want cheaper housing overall, you're a business owner who can work the franchise tax angle, or you're in a high-growth area where property values are going up fast but you plan to sell before the tax bills catch up.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAnd both states are a \\u003cem\\u003elot\\u003c/em\\u003e cheaper than California, New York, or Illinois. Like, not even close. That part isn't even a debate.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse our \\u003cstrong\\u003erelocation calculator\\u003c/strong\\u003e to see your personalized savings from moving to either state.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"21:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-4 mb-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium bg-cyan-100 text-cyan-800 dark:bg-cyan-500/20 dark:text-cyan-400 border-cyan-300 dark:border-cyan-500/30\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Comparison\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-amber-300 bg-amber-100 px-2.5 py-0.5